# Every refusal branch no test has ever entered, and why it is allowed to stay.
#
# `.github/scripts/uncovered-guards.sh check` derives the refusal set from the
# source tree (`.github/scripts/guardsites`), merges the coverage profiles of
# every lane in .github/build-tags.manifest, and compares the two against this
# file in both directions: uncovered in the tree but missing here is red, and
# listed here while a test now enters the branch -- or while the branch is gone
# -- is red too.
#
# Nothing declares which branches are guards. The set comes out of the tree, and
# this file lists only the waivers, so a new guard with no test cannot land
# quietly: there is no register to forget to update. That inversion is the whole
# point -- see the design record on BEO-63, "guard enforcement: derived, not
# declared". A register OF guards fails open at exactly the moment it has to
# fail closed.
#
# Equal identity sets and pure shrink pass as guards gain coverage or leave the
# tree. A recognizer expansion may expose pre-existing sites, but pure growth is
# admitted only when every added row is explicitly reviewed and marked
# `growth(#[1-9][0-9]*): <nonblank reason>`; undisclosed growth and every mixed
# addition/removal fail. The reason column is self-declared, so a waiver nobody
# would accept keeps this green -- reading the diff is what catches that, not the
# script. Missing or malformed current or exact-base data fails closed.
#
# Data rows are exactly five tab-separated columns:
# file <TAB> func <TAB> nth <TAB> predicate <TAB> reason. Every field is
# non-empty, and `nth` must be a positive decimal ordinal. `predicate` is the if
# condition, or the literal `default` for a refusing switch default. The same
# format validation applies to the exact base baseline used by the ratchet.
#
# `nth` is the 1-based occurrence of that exact predicate in that owner identity;
# see `.github/scripts/guardsites` for the owner forms and derivation contract.
# No line numbers on purpose -- they are not part of an entry's identity, and
# including them would rewrite this file on every unrelated edit above a guard.
# Run `.github/scripts/uncovered-guards.sh classify` for the current verdicts.
#
# A few entries are marked GOOS-gated. Coverage cannot be unioned across
# platforms the way deadcode.sh unions reachability -- tests cannot run for a
# GOOS they were not built for -- so a guard in a file the runner does not
# compile is measured on one platform and not the other. Those lines are carried
# for both platforms and ignored by whichever one cannot measure them.

internal/cli/report_cmd.go	enforceScoutReportBudget	1	agg.LaunchEvidence.SubmittedAt <= 0	unreachable because validateAggregate rejects SubmittedAt <= 0 on the READ path (canonical.go readTaskDoc), so auth.Get never serves such a record; the only writer, Canonical.RecordLaunch, additionally stamps it from the authority clock. Wrong the moment a read path skips validateAggregate, or validateLaunchEvidence stops checking SubmittedAt. Premise pinned by TestPremiseGetRefusesLaunchEvidenceWithNoSubmissionTimestamp, which writes a task doc carrying SubmittedAt = 0 straight to the store and asserts auth.Get refuses it
